    <li><strong>Downward trend:</strong> The global median homicide rate fell from approximately {earliest_median:.1f} per 100,000 in the early 1990s to {latest_median:.1f} per 100,000 by {most_recent_year} — a {abs(trend_pct):.0f}% decline.</li>
    <li><strong>Mean-median gap:</strong> The mean consistently exceeds the median, indicating a right-skewed distribution driven by a subset of countries with very high homicide rates.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting expansion:</strong> The number of countries reporting data has grown substantially over the period (from {int(global_trend.iloc[0]['count'])} in {int(global_trend.iloc[0]['year'])} to {int(global_trend.iloc[-1]['count'])} in {int(global_trend.iloc[-1]['year'])}), which adds complexity to inter-temporal comparisons.</li>
    <li><strong>Post-2020 stability:</strong> The most recent years show relative stabilization, though the limited number of reporting countries in 2020–2023 warrants cautious interpretation.</li>
  </ul>

