  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart4_b64}" alt="Age Group Homicide Rates Over Time">
  </div>

  <h3>Findings</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Youth and young adults (15–29) consistently face the highest homicide risk</strong>, with rates substantially above the population average. This pattern holds across nearly all countries and time periods.</li>
    <li><strong>The 30–44 age group ranks second</strong>, followed by the 45–59 group.</li>
    <li><strong>Children (0–14) and the elderly (60+) have the lowest rates</strong>, though child homicide is a particular concern due to its severity and the vulnerability of victims.</li>
    <li><strong>Temporal trend:</strong> All age groups have experienced some decline over the observation period, but the relative ranking of age groups has remained stable — the young adult peak is a structural feature of homicide victimization globally.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Policy Implication:</strong> The disproportionate victimization of the 15–29 age group underscores the need for targeted youth violence prevention programs, including community-based interventions, educational and employment opportunities, and firearms regulation where applicable.
  </div>
