  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart6_b64}" alt="Outlier Country Trajectories">
  </div>

  <h3>Contextual Notes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dramatic declines</strong> often coincide with improvements in policing, disarmament programs, peace processes, or socioeconomic development. Countries like El Salvador and Colombia have implemented aggressive anti-gang and counter-narcotics policies that have yielded measurable, if sometimes contested, results.</li>
    <li><strong>Dramatic increases</strong> frequently correspond to periods of political instability, conflict, or institutional breakdown. The magnitude of some increases underscores the fragility of security gains.</li>
    <li><strong>Reversibility:</strong> The presence of both large declines and increases in the same period demonstrates that homicide trends are not unidirectional — gains can be reversed, and deteriorations can be arrested with appropriate intervention.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>Statistical Caution:</strong> Percentage changes can appear extreme when starting rates are very low. A country moving from 0.5 to 2.0 per 100,000 represents a 300% increase that is numerically dramatic but may reflect different dynamics than a country moving from 20 to 60 per 100,000. Always consider absolute rates alongside percentage changes.
  </div>
