<div class="section" id="data-limitations">
  <h2>6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>

  <h3>Data Limitations</h3>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart7_b64}" alt="Reporting Coverage Over Time">
  </div>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Unbalanced panel:</strong> This dataset is fundamentally an unbalanced panel — not all countries report data for every year. The set of countries included in global averages changes over time, which complicates trend interpretation. A declining global average could partly reflect the addition of lower-rate countries to the reporting pool.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting gaps:</strong> Coverage varies significantly by region and year. In the earliest years, fewer than {int(coverage_by_year.min())} countries reported, while recent years have seen over {int(coverage_by_year.max())}. This expansion of coverage improves representativeness but creates comparability challenges.</li>
    <li><strong>Underreporting:</strong> Even when countries report, the data may not capture all homicides. Weak criminal justice systems, political incentives to undercount, and the challenges of documenting deaths in conflict zones all contribute to potential undercounting.</li>
    <li><strong>United Kingdom caveat:</strong> UK data refer to England and Wales only, as reported in the original UNODC dataset. Scotland and Northern Ireland may appear as separate entries.</li>
    <li><strong>Territories vs. countries:</strong> Some territories (e.g., Hong Kong, Macao, Puerto Rico) are included separately and should not be double-counted with their parent countries in aggregate analyses.</li>
    <li><strong>No imputation:</strong> Missing years were not interpolated. All computed averages and trends use only observed data points.</li>
